Acute Limb Ischaemia (Upper)
Acute Urgent Care
Sudden severe arm pain, Absent radial/brachial pulses, Acute hand pallor/coldness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-008
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe, unrelenting pain in the right arm accompanied by coldness and loss of sensation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a sudden onset of excruciating arm pain, pallor, and numbness, strongly suggesting an acute arterial occlusion. Urgent face-to-face assessment was critical because skeletal muscle and nerve tissue suffer irreversible necrosis within 4 to 6 hours of complete ischemia. Waiting for a routine morning appointment would have guaranteed permanent limb loss.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My right arm went completely numb and freezing cold an hour ago, and the pain is absolutely agonizing.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the sudden onset of severe, unremitting right arm pain that started approximately 90 minutes ago while watching television. The pain is localized distal to the elbow. The patient noticed immediate pallor, describing the hand as a waxy, white color that is now progressing to mottling. There is a perishingly cold sensation to the touch. The patient complains of a severe pins-and-needles sensation that is rapidly transitioning into complete numbness over the fingers. Weakness in gripping objects is now apparent. The patient has a known history of atrial fibrillation.
Functional Status: Distressed and pacing the room; unable to use the right arm.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; unable to drive due to lack of arm function and severe pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 115 (Irregular) | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient is clutching the right arm, pacing, visibly anxious and in severe pain.
Physical Exam: The right hand and distal forearm are profoundly pale, with a waxy appearance and early mottling. There is a complete absence of palpable radial, ulnar, and brachial pulses on the right side. The limb is perishingly cold to the touch with a clear transverse line of temperature demarcation at the mid-forearm. Superficial veins are completely collapsed. Neurological exam reveals significant sensory loss over the digits and sudden motor weakness affecting the intrinsic muscles of the hand. Cardiac auscultation confirms an irregular heartbeat consistent with atrial fibrillation. Expected investigations (arterial duplex or CT angiography) will likely demonstrate an abrupt cessation of blood flow in the brachial artery.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathway for emergency responders.
Medication Reconciliation: Prescribed Apixaban, but patient admits to missing doses for the last three days due to a lost prescription.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Limb Ischaemia (Upper) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Cervical Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absent pulses, profound pallor, and temperature demarcation, which indicate a vascular emergency rather than nerve root compression.

Deep Vein Thrombosis (Upper Extremity) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the limb being pale, cold, and pulseless, whereas a DVT typically presents with a warm, swollen, and erythematous limb with intact arterial pulses.

Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of preceding major trauma or fracture, and the primary issue being absent inflow rather than trapped swelling.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation perfectly matches the classic signs of acute limb ischemia: Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesia, Perishing cold, and Paralysis (the 6 Ps). Given the known history of atrial fibrillation and missed anticoagulant doses, a cardiogenic embolus to the brachial artery is the definitive working diagnosis. An urgent after-hours assessment was unequivocally required, as the window for successful embolic retrieval and limb salvage is narrow (typically under 6 hours). Any delay in medical evaluation and subsequent vascular surgery referral would have resulted in irreversible muscle necrosis and highly probable amputation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered strong oral analgesia (Oxycodone 5mg) immediately. Called 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport to a center with vascular surgery capabilities. Kept the limb in a dependent position.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your blood thinner medication exactly as prescribed every single day without missing a dose, you maintain a steady shield in your bloodstream that stops these dangerous clots from forming in your heart and traveling to your limbs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics for immediate ED transfer.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The patient is to remain seated with the arm resting below heart level. If the patient develops sudden chest pain or facial drooping, alert the 000 operator immediately as the clot source may shower other arteries.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a critical blockage of the blood supply to your arm requiring immediate hospital imaging and likely a surgical procedure to remove the clot.
Clinical Course: If blood flow is restored quickly, the arm can fully recover. Delaying treatment leads to permanent nerve damage and tissue death.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep your arm hanging down by your side to let gravity help whatever trickle of blood is left reach your fingers. Do not elevate the arm and do not apply any heat packs, as the numb skin will burn severely.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have given you a strong painkiller, but the primary treatment is getting to the hospital where a surgeon can physically remove the clot that is blocking the artery.
Actionable Steps: Sit in this chair with your arm pointing toward the floor. Keep your house keys in your good hand so you can open the door for the paramedics the moment they arrive.

